        <fig id="article-169387.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Benjamin-Inglis Grading System for Laryngeal Clefts.&#x000a0;Type I is purely interarytenoid, and type II extends into the posterior cricoid plate but not completely through it. Type III fully splits the posterior cricoid plate but does not extend past the thoracic inlet, and type IV extends beyond the thoracic inlet. Henry Vandyke Carter, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?search=Henry+Vandyke+Carter&#x00026;title=Special:MediaSearch&#x00026;go=Go&#x00026;type=image&#x00026;haslicense=unrestricted">Public Domain</ext-link>, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Benjamin-Inglis" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-169387.s15">
        <fig id="article-169387.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Diagnostic Laryngoscopy of a Type I Laryngeal Cleft. The cleft is palpated with a probe to examine its extent. Contributed by R De Jong, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="LC__diagnostic__DL__with__probe" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
